From: gcc-for-windows98 patcher <patches@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] xargs --parallel: TerminateProcess on Win9x Ctrl+C

ctrl_handler's parallel-mode branch called
`kill(GetProcessId(G.procs[i]), SIGTERM)` on each running child.
Win9x's GetProcessId returns 0 via shim and signal-by-handle uses
CreateRemoteThread (NT-only), so on Win9x the parallel xargs
Ctrl+C handler is a no-op and children outlive the parent.

On Win9x, TerminateProcess() the HANDLE directly.  Loses
descendants of each child but kills the immediate child --
the property a SIGINT user is after when interrupting xargs.

Fixes audit site #3 from repro/docs/win9x-shim-audit.md.
Single-process Ctrl+C path (kill(-pid)) remains degraded;
noted in the audit, low priority.

---
diff --git a/findutils/xargs.c b/findutils/xargs.c
--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -210,9 +210,18 @@
 			int i;
 
 			for (i = 0; i < G.running_procs; ++i) {
-				pid_t pid = GetProcessId(G.procs[i]);
-				if (pid > 0)
-					kill(-pid, SIGTERM);
+				if (is_win9x_proc()) {
+					/* GetProcessId is Vista+ (returns 0 via shim);
+					 * kill_signal_by_handle uses CreateRemoteThread
+					 * (NT-only).  TerminateProcess directly on the
+					 * HANDLE -- loses descendants but kills the
+					 * immediate child so it doesn't outlive xargs. */
+					TerminateProcess(G.procs[i], SIGTERM << 24);
+				} else {
+					pid_t pid = GetProcessId(G.procs[i]);
+					if (pid > 0)
+						kill(-pid, SIGTERM);
+				}
 			}
 		}
 # endif
